<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Przemysł II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1257–<a href="/years/1296">1296</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Przemysł II was duke of Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> who gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> in 1294 and was crowned King of Poland at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a> in 1295, the first royal coronation in Poland for over two centuries. He was murdered at Rogoźno in February 1296, probably by agents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, and his lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek">Władysław the Elbow-high</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii.jpg" alt="Przemysł II" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous plate · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Przemys%C5%82_II_King_of_Poland_from_1296.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Przemysł II was born on 14 October 1257 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a>, the only son of Duke Przemysł I of Greater Poland and Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, and was born after his father's death. His uncle, Duke Bolesław the Pious, took guardianship of the duchy, and after his mother died in 1265 Przemysł and his sisters were raised in their uncle's care. In 1272 he was named nominal commander of an expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> that captured the fortress of Strzelce Krajeńskie. He married Ludgarda, a granddaughter of Barnim I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, in 1273, as part of an alliance with Pomerania.</p><p>In 1273 he became independent Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a>, though the circumstances are unclear. One document suggests he was held under house arrest in the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a> and escaped without help. He then allied with Henry Probus, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>. On the death of Bolesław the Pious in 1279 he also obtained the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a> and ruled all Greater Poland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In these years he worked with Jakub Świnka, whom he saw consecrated as Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a> on 18 December 1283, to unite the Piast principalities. In 1281 Henry Probus broke the rules of hospitality by imprisoning him at a meeting and forcing political concessions. A rebellion by Sędziwój Zaremba in 1284 cost him the castle at Ołobok for a time. Under the will of Henry Probus he received the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> in 1290, with the title of High Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, but he lacked the support of the local nobility and withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>, taking the royal crown and regalia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a> with him.</p><p>He allied in 1293 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek">Władysław the Elbow-high</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-ii-of-leczyca">Casimir II of Łęczyca</a> against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a>, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestwin-ii">Mestwin II</a> died in 1294, Przemysł inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kepno">Treaty of Kępno</a> of 1282. This made him the strongest ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a> and enabled his coronation as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a> on 26 June 1295, performed by Jakub Świnka. He was the first Polish ruler to hold the hereditary royal title after a long period of high dukes and two nominal kings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Only nine months after his coronation, on 8 February 1296, Przemysł was murdered during a failed kidnapping attempt by men of the Margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, with some help from the noble families of Nałęcz and Zaremba. Brandenburg had meant to seize him, not to kill him. He was the last male of the Greater Poland line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a>. His death brought rapid intervention by those who wished to take over his lands, and by March Greater Poland was torn between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek">Władysław the Elbow-high</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-glogow">Henry III of Głogów</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Przemysł II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Przemysł II</b>, Duke of Greater Poland, was crowned King of Poland in Gniezno Cathedral, the first royal coronation in Poland for over two centuries.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-26">26 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Przemysl II</b> gained Pomerelia in 1294 and was crowned King of Poland in Gniezno in 1295.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Duchy of Greater Poland</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Mestwin II, who died without sons, left the duchy to <b>Przemysl II</b> of Greater Poland under the Treaty of Kepno of 1282.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerelia">Duchy of Pomerelia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Rogoźno, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.41,16.93&amp;year=1282" aria-label="Poznań on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4930 355 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Przemysł II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.41,16.93&amp;year=1282">What was here in 1282? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
